    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1928, Elihue Coldiron opened the town’s first post office at the mouth of Jesse’s Branch, honoring his family’s legacy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/coldiron_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Louisville and Nashville Railroad’s 1911 extension up the Cumberland River spurred timber and coal development in Coldiron.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/coldiron_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Coldiron sits in the Cumberland Corridor, stretching along the river between Layman and Wallins Creek, near Wallins Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
